<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-implicit-differentiation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Implicit Differentiation</title>
  
  <!-- <introduction>
  <p>
  This is the introduction to the section.
  </p>
  </introduction> -->
  
  <subsection xml:id="sec-implicit-curves-slopes">
    <title>Implicit Curves and Their Slopes</title>
    
    <p>The slope of the curve <m>y = f(x)</m> is given by the derivative <m>f'(x)</m>. However, not all curves are defined by <term>explicit</term> functions, where <m>y</m> is isolated and is given as a function of <m>x</m>. Some functions are <term>implicit</term>, defined by a relation between <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. For example,</p>
    <md>
      <mrow>\begin{array}{c|c} \text{explicit} \amp \text{implicit} \\ \hline y = 2x + 5 \amp 2x + 3y = 6 \\ y = \sin{x} + 4 \amp x^2 + y^2 = 4 \end{array}</mrow>
    </md>
    <p>Basically,</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><term>Explicit</term> means <m>y</m> is isolated.</p></li>
      <li><p><term>Implicit</term> means the <m>y</m> is not isolated, so the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> terms are mixed together.</p></li>
    </ul>
    
    <example xml:id="ex-circle-intro">
      <p>For example, <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/ofb03pr4aq">a circle with radius 1 centered at the origin</url> (the unit circle) has the equation,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>x^2 + y^2 = 1</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>For the entire graph, this is not a function, because it fails the vertical line test. However, it is still something which we could create tangent lines for.</p>
      <p>We want to take derivatives of these kinds of curves. To do so, one strategy would be to isolate <m>y</m>, and then take the derivative like we have done previously. In this case,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>x^2 + y^2 \amp = 1</mrow>
        <mrow>y^2 \amp = 1 - x^2 \amp\amp \text{subtracting $x^2$ from both sides}</mrow>
        <mrow>y \amp = \pm \sqrt{1-x^2} \amp\amp \text{taking the square root of both sides}</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>This leads to two different functions, say <m>y_1(x) = \sqrt{1-x^2}</m> and <m>y_2(x) = - \sqrt{1-x^2}</m>. The first is for the top semicircle, and the second is for the bottom semicircle (<url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vy5ynpljey">Desmos link</url>).</p>
    </example>
    
    <p>For some equations, it is possible to solve for <m>y</m> as an explicit function (or possibly multiple functions) of <m>x</m>. However, in many other more complicated cases, this is difficult or actually algebraically impossible.</p>
    <p>In fact, it is not necessary to solve for <m>y</m> explicitly to find the derivative. Instead, we can use a technique called <em>implicit differentiation</em>.</p>
    
    <example xml:id="ex-motivational-circle">
      <title>Motivational Example: Circle</title>
      <p>Consider the unit circle, with equation <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>. The idea is that we can take the derivative of <em>both</em> sides of the equation,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\frac{d}{dx} (x^2 + y^2) \amp = \frac{d}{dx} (1)</mrow>
        <mrow>\frac{d}{dx}(x^2) + \frac{d}{dx} (y^2) \amp = \frac{d}{dx}(1)</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>As usual,</p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><m>\frac{d}{dx} (x^2) = 2x</m> (power rule) and,</p></li>
        <li><p><m>\frac{d}{dx}(1) = 0</m> (derivative of a constant is zero)</p></li>
      </ul>
      <p>However, <m>\frac{d}{dx}(y^2)</m> is a bit different, because <m>y</m> represents a function of <m>x</m>, so instead of <m>y</m>, we could more precisely write <m>y(x)</m>, like,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\frac{d}{dx} (y(x))^2</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>This means that <m>y^2</m> is like a composition of function, with <m>y</m> as the inner function, and the square is the outer function. This means we have to use the chain rule,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\frac{d}{dx} (y(x))^2 = \underbrace{2 \cdot y(x)}_{\text{power rule}} \cdot \underbrace{\frac{dy}{dx}}_{\text{chain rule}}</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>Then, the equation becomes,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>2x + 2y \frac{dy}{dx} = 0</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>Then, we want to solve for the derivative <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m>, so we can isolate it in the equation,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>2y \frac{dy}{dx} \amp = -2x</mrow>
        <mrow>\frac{dy}{dx} \amp = \frac{-2x}{2y}</mrow>
        <mrow>\frac{dy}{dx} \amp = -\frac{x}{y}</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>This means that for any point <m>(x,y)</m> on the unit circle, the slope of the tangent line at that point is <m>-\frac{x}{y}</m>. For example, for the point <m>(x,y) = (\frac{3}{5}, \frac{4}{5})</m>, the derivative (slope) at that point is,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\frac{dy}{dx} = -\frac{3/5}{4/5} = -\frac{3}{4}</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>Note that <m>y</m> is included in the formula for the derivative (not just <m>x</m>), and this is totally normal and ok.</p>
      <p>Also, we can use the derivative formula to find where the graph has a horizontal tangent, and also where it has a vertical tangent.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>There is a horizontal tangent if <m>y' = 0</m>, or <m>-\frac{x}{y} = 0</m>. This means that <m>x = 0</m>. Plugging this into the curve equation <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>, we get <m>y^2 = 1</m>, so <m>y = \pm 1</m>. So, <m>(0,1)</m> and <m>(0,-1)</m>. This should make sense graphically.</p></li>
        <li><p>For vertical tangents, this is basically where the derivative is undefined, because of division by 0. Here, it is when <m>y = 0</m>. Plugging this into the equation, we get <m>x^2 = 1</m>, so <m>x = \pm 1</m>, and so the points are <m>(1,0)</m> and <m>(-1,0)</m>.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </example>
    
    <example xml:id="ex-horizontal-parabolas">
      <title>Horizontal Parabolas</title>
      <p>Consider the equation <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/rfcgm604pw"><m>y^2 = x</m></url> (or <m>x = y^2</m>). This equation represents a horizontal parabola. We could solve this equation for <m>y</m>, by taking the square root of both sides to get <m>y = \pm \sqrt{x}</m>. In other words, this defines two functions of <m>x</m>, <m>y_1(x) = \sqrt{x}</m> and <m>y_2(x) = -\sqrt{x}</m>. We could take the derivative of each individually, to get,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>y_1'(x) = \frac{1}{2\sqrt{x}} \qquad \text{and} \qquad y_2'(x) = -\frac{1}{2\sqrt{x}}</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>Instead, using implicit differentiation, we can take the derivative of both together. Differentiate both sides of the equation,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\frac{d}{dx}(y^2) \amp = \frac{d}{dx}(x)</mrow>
        <mrow>2y \frac{dy}{dx} \amp = 1</mrow>
        <mrow>\frac{dy}{dx} \amp = \frac{1}{2y} \quad \text{solving for $\frac{dy}{dx}$}</mrow>
      </md>
    </example>
    
    <example xml:id="ex-folium-descartes">
      <title>Folium of Descartes</title>
      <p>The equation <m>x^3 + y^3 = 3xy</m> represents a curve called the <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/epbpe0lbki"><em>folium of Descartes</em></url> (<q>folium</q> is Latin for <q>leaf</q>). Notice that <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are mixed together, so this is an implicit equation. It is technically possible to solve for <m>y</m> in this formula, however this requires advanced techniques, and the explicit expression is very complicated, making the derivative tedious to compute. Instead, using implicit differentiation, take the derivative of both sides,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\frac{d}{dx} \brac{x^3 + y^3} \amp = \frac{d}{dx}(3xy)</mrow>
        <mrow>3x^2 + 3y^2 \cdot y' \amp = 3xy' + 3y</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>Then, solving for <m>y'</m>,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>3y^2 y' - 3xy' \amp = 3y - 3x^2</mrow>
        <mrow>(3y^2 - 3x) y' \amp = 3y - 3x^2</mrow>
        <mrow>y' \amp = \frac{3y - 3x^2}{3y^2 - 3x}</mrow>
        <mrow>y' \amp = \frac{y - x^2}{y^2 - x}</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>Then, for example, for the point <m>(1,2)</m> on the curve (you can verify that <m>(1,2)</m> is indeed on the curve, by plugging it into the equation <m>x^3 + y^3 = 3xy</m>, and verifying that both sides are equal),</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>y' = \frac{2 - 1^2}{2^2 - 1} = \frac{1}{3}</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>Then, the equation of the tangent line at <m>(1,2)</m> is,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>y - 2 = \frac{1}{3}(x-1)</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>We can also consider where on this curve has a horizontal tangent line. This occurs when <m>y' = 0</m>, or,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\frac{y - x^2}{y^2 - x} \amp = 0</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>This occurs when the numerator is equal to 0, or <m>y - x^2 = 0</m>, or <m>y = x^2</m>. This doesn't give a particular point, but instead a relationship between <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>. Combining this with the original curve equation, this forms like a system of equations,</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>\begin{cases} y - x^2 = 0 \amp \text{for $y' = 0$} \\ x^3 + y^3 = 3xy \amp \text{for the point to be on the curve} \end{cases}</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>Then, solving this will give us the points where the tangent line is horizontal.</p>
      <md>
        <mrow>x^3 + (x^2)^3 \amp = 3x (x^2)</mrow>
        <mrow>x^3 + x^6 \amp = 3x^3</mrow>
        <mrow>x^6 - 2x^3 \amp = 0</mrow>
        <mrow>x^3(x^3 - 2) \amp = 0</mrow>
        <mrow>x = 0 \qquad \amp x = \sqrt[3]{2}</mrow>
      </md>
      <p>Then, substituting back into the equation <m>y = x^2</m>, we get <m>y = 0</m> and <m>y = \sqrt[3]{4}</m>. Therefore, there is a horizontal tangent at <m>(0,0)</m> and <m>(\sqrt[3]{2}, \sqrt[3]{4})</m>.</p>
    </example>
  </subsection>
  
  <subsection xml:id="sec-summary-implicit-diff">
    <title>Summary of Implicit Differentiation</title>
    <p>In summary, to do implicit differentiation:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><p>Differentiate both sides (with respect to <m>x</m>), i.e. <q>take <m>\frac{d}{dx}</m> of both sides</q>.</p></li>
      <li>
        <p>When differentiating any term involving <m>y</m>, treat <m>y</m> as a function of <m>x</m>, and use the chain rule (basically, multiply by <m>y'</m> at the end). Keep in mind that,</p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\frac{d}{dx} x = 1 \qquad \text{and} \qquad \frac{d}{dx} y = y'</mrow>
        </md>
      </li>
      <li><p>Solve for <m>y'</m> (isolate <m>y'</m>) in the equation.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <p>Some notes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>You can use either <m>y'</m> or <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m> to represent the derivative of <m>y</m> with respect to <m>x</m>. The latter notation <m>y'</m> is a bit more compact.</p></li>
      <li><p>With implicit differentiation, the derivative <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m> is usually in terms of both <m>x</m> <em>and</em> <m>y</m>. This means, to evaluate the derivative at a particular point, it is necessary to know both coordinates. In contrast, the derivative of an explicit function only requires <m>x</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>We can think of the derivative <m>\frac{d}{dx}</m> as an operator that we can apply to both sides of the equation. This is analogous to how we can square both sides, or take the logarithm of both sides.</p></li>
      <li><p>Implicit differentiation also has the advantage that it leads to one formula for the derivative, that applies to all points on the graph, even if the graph may have 2 or 3 or even more separate function equations.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </subsection>
  
  <subsection xml:id="sec-horiz-vert-tangents">
    <title>Horizontal and Vertical Tangents</title>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Horizontal tangents occur when <m>y' = 0</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Vertical tangents occur when <m>y'</m> is undefined.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p>For a curve with <m>y' = \frac{\text{numerator}}{\text{denominator}}</m>,</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Horizontal tangents when <m>\text{numerator} = 0</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Vertical tangents when <m>\text{denominator} = 0</m>.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Note: technically, if both the numerator and denominator are 0, then this is a separate case and the regular rules don't apply.</p>
  </subsection>
  
</section>